WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.000
In real-world projects, could you go through and describe a little bit on when you would use a computer to property versus a watcher?

00:10.000 --> 00:17.000
So the question here is around taking more real-world example of why computer properties would be advantageous over watchers.

00:17.000 --> 00:26.000
So in the example of a data visualization model, I think that still remains probably one of the better examples of this,

00:26.000 --> 00:32.000
in that if you were to try and take a look at any sort of SVG animation out there chart.

00:37.000 --> 00:44.000
You'll know that for something like this, there are a lot of pieces that go into the computation of how this is being drawn.

00:44.000 --> 00:51.000
It could be as simple as how big the pie chart is being drawn or what the data that goes into whether or not it's being filled, what color it might be.

00:51.000 --> 00:53.000
There's a lot that's happening here.

00:53.000 --> 01:04.000
And so if you try to watch all your dependencies individually, what people have found when they worked with these larger code bases is that if they weren't careful with how they tracked their dependencies,

01:04.000 --> 01:07.000
they could trigger re-renders in their code when they didn't expect to.

01:07.000 --> 01:14.000
And for those who have had experience working on enterprise apps, there is almost no way you'll own the code base from end to end.

01:14.000 --> 01:19.000
So a lot of times you don't even know that someone is depending on the thing that you're writing for.

01:20.000 --> 01:33.000
And so computer properties have the advantage of, because the chains are being tracked basically explicitly, when you use computer properties,

01:33.000 --> 01:37.000
not only are you containing the dependencies, which I understand watch does do,

01:37.000 --> 01:44.000
because if we think about it, when we did watch, you have to actually define the initial value that you're watching.

01:44.000 --> 01:48.000
So in this case, let's say it was characterless.

01:48.000 --> 02:02.000
And then on top of that, can you imagine what happens if this also had this.characterList is dependent on this.favoriteList?

02:02.000 --> 02:07.000
Once you start adding multiple things, especially when you're receiving data from an API,

02:07.000 --> 02:10.000
it becomes really difficult to start tracking where everything is.

02:10.000 --> 02:17.000
And so computer properties, because you can basically just throw all your dependencies inside of a single compute function,

02:17.000 --> 02:24.000
it has the algorithm that's required for you to automatically do all the performance optimizations and caching that you would want to do.

02:24.000 --> 02:26.000
I think that's the best way I could explain it at this point.

02:26.000 --> 02:31.000
And so remember, the key thing here is not that watch is bad, is that watch is good for triggering side effects.

02:31.000 --> 02:38.000
And computed, it's kind of like when you import load-ash and you do a debounce function on an API call because you're afraid users will click.

02:38.000 --> 02:43.000
Imagine computed has a debounce built in so that things don't automatically trigger.

02:43.000 --> 02:47.000
It knows how to protect you from those things. That's the advantage of computed.

